<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Three months of winter wheeze, cough and breathlessness in a 19-year-old never-smoking woman, born in West Africa, who has been called frail and asthmatic since childhood but has never had lung function testing or treatment. Please confirm or refute asthma with spirometry before and after bronchodilator and FeNO, help exclude other chest disease, and advise on treatment, including preparation for a long sea journey she may make in the spring. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does she have asthma, and have tuberculosis and other chest disease been excluded?</item><item>How should her lung function be interpreted, given the move to race-neutral reference equations?</item><item>Which GINA track and step do you recommend, and what reliever?</item><item>What is her inflammatory phenotype, and which triggers matter most?</item><item>How should she prepare for a long sea journey and months away from her clinic?</item><item>What should she know about her asthma treatment if she becomes pregnant in the future?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Miss Wheatley, 19, is seen at the request of Dr. Hartley-Okonkwo for three months of winter wheeze, cough and breathlessness, on a background of a weak chest since childhood that was called asthma but never tested. Symptoms come on three or four days a week, with waking about twice a month, and are brought on by cold wind, kitchen and fireplace smoke, and colds. She has never smoked, has never had an inhaler, and has never needed emergency care for her breathing. She may travel by sea in the spring. She was seen alone and gave her own consent. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Phillis Wheatley was born in West Africa, probably in the Senegambia region (present-day Senegal and the Gambia), about 1753 or 1754. Nothing is known of her early life before she was kidnapped and trafficked as a child. [D]</item><item>In 1761 the child arrived in Boston aboard the slave ship Phillis (on 11 July, by one scholarly chronology) and was bought by the Boston merchant John Wheatley and his wife Susanna (or Susannah), in whose household she was enslaved as a domestic servant and companion. She was named Phillis after the ship and Wheatley after the family. The family judged her to be about seven because she was shedding her front teeth. [D]</item><item>When she was bought in 1761 she was described as a slender, frail child, &quot;evidently suffering from a change of climate&quot;; the slave ship&apos;s captain is said to have sold her cheaply because he believed she was dying. One reference work describes her as &quot;the frail, asthmatic child&quot; and says she remained small and delicate. [D]</item><item>In the Wheatley household she was taught to read and write and within about sixteen months could read the Bible; she went on to read Greek and Latin classics and British literature. Her first known poem was published in the Newport Mercury in December 1767, and her elegy on the evangelist George Whitefield, published in 1770, brought her wide renown. [D]</item><item>Proposals to publish a volume of her poems in Boston in 1772 found too few subscribers, and the volume was instead prepared for publication in London with the support of Selina Hastings, Countess of Huntingdon. [D]</item></list><paragraph>8 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

