<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Asthma, confirmed, mild persistent, with type 2 inflammation (J45.30). Question 1: this is asthma. The FEV1/FVC below the lower limit of normal that rises to normal after albuterol, with a 330 mL (15 percent) rise in FEV1, shows variable, reversible airflow obstruction. Tuberculosis infection is excluded by the negative interferon-gamma release assay, and the clear chest X-ray argues against bronchiectasis or scarring; nothing in her history suggests inducible laryngeal obstruction. Question 2: with race-neutral GLI Global equations her FEV1 is 83 percent of predicted; older race-based equations would have set a lower predicted value and could have made her obstruction look like none at all. Question 4: FeNO 38 ppb, blood eosinophils of 0.38 and sensitization to house dust mite show type 2 inflammation, which responds well to inhaled corticosteroid. Her most important triggers are cold air, smoke from cooking and fires, and colds; dust mite matters in the bedroom. [S]</item><item>Wheeze, cough and breathlessness (R06.2). Explained by asthma. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Hartley-Okonkwo&apos;s intake note and results: chest X-ray clear; blood eosinophils 0.38; interferon-gamma release assay negative; urine pregnancy test negative; Asthma Control Test 15. This morning, in our laboratory, with GLI Global race-neutral equations: FEV1 2.24 L (83 percent predicted) before and 2.57 L after albuterol 400 mcg (+330 mL, +15 percent); FEV1/FVC 0.72 before, below the lower limit of normal of 0.75, and 0.80 after; FeNO 38 ppb. Total IgE 165 IU/mL; specific IgE positive to house dust mite and negative to cat and mold. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Question 3: GINA track 1, step 2: budesonide-formoterol 160/4.5 mcg, one inhalation as needed for symptoms and before going out in the cold, up to 12 a day; no albuterol-only reliever. This halves the risk of severe exacerbations compared with albuterol alone and suits her symptoms on several days a week. [S]</item><item>Teach technique with a spacer and check it at every visit; review six weeks after starting and within a week of any exacerbation; step up to maintenance and reliever (step 3) if control is not reached or after an exacerbation, and step back down when she is well. [S]</item><item>A written action plan with peak flow zones, and a two-week diary to set her personal best. [S]</item><item>Triggers: ventilate the kitchen and avoid fireplace smoke; a scarf over the mouth in cold wind; mite-proof bedding covers. [S]</item><item>Question 5: for the journey, two inhalers and the spacer, zones set from her personal best, a standby five-day course of prednisone with clear start rules for when care is far away, treatment for seasickness, an aired cabin away from smoke, and a printed summary; spirometry and FeNO again after her return. [S]</item><item>Question 6: well-controlled asthma is safe in pregnancy and uncontrolled asthma is the main risk; budesonide is the inhaled steroid with the most reassuring pregnancy data, and her treatment should continue, not stop, if she becomes pregnant; plan a review before and during any pregnancy. [S]</item><item>Vaccines: influenza and COVID-19 each season, and PCV20 once, as an adult with asthma. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Hartley-Okonkwo the same day. The results were explained to Miss Wheatley alone, in plain words, with a printed copy; she asked how the reference equations work and why it matters. She decides who else may receive this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Pulmonology review after the repeat spirometry on her return, or sooner if an exacerbation occurs or control is not reached; Dr. Hartley-Okonkwo continues primary care and shares the monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>
